# The Median Line License (MLL) — Draft v0.3

*A trust-based software license that is free until you are doing better than
half of your neighbors, and asks for a fair share only of what the software
actually helped you earn — never enough to pull you back below the line.*

---

## Preamble — read this first

Most software licensing makes you choose: give your work away to everyone
(including people who will profit enormously from it and return nothing), or
charge everyone (including people who cannot afford it and needed it most).

This license refuses that choice. Its principle is simple:

> **Use this software freely while you are getting started. Once it has
> genuinely helped you rise above the median income of the place you live,
> contribute a small share of what it helped you earn — and never so much
> that paying would pull you back below that line.**

The median is the heart of the design. It is local, so a licensee in rural
Mississippi and a licensee in Manhattan are each measured against their own
neighbors, not against a single national number. And it moves with the
economy — in hard times the line falls and more people qualify for free use;
in good times it rises with everyone's fortunes. The license scales itself.

**This is a draft, and it is honest about being one.** It has not yet been
reviewed by an attorney (Appendix A lists the questions such review should
answer). It has no audits, no reporting requirements, no enforcement
machinery. It runs on the good faith of the people who use the software —
deliberately. If you are the kind of person who would cheat an honor-system
license, no contract language was ever going to stop you anyway. If you are
the kind of person who wouldn't, welcome.

The license **text itself** is dedicated to the public domain (Section 13)
so that any developer, lawyer, or economist can adopt it, harden it, and
improve it. If this idea is good, it should outgrow any single project.

---

## Parameters

This license is a reusable template. Each project that adopts it fills in
this block. For **this** project:

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---|
| **The Work** | VFINX vs Trend (vfinx-trend) (source code, documentation, scripts, and assets in this repository) |
| **Licensor** | Tim O'Donnell, and their successors and assigns |
| **Copyright** | Copyright © 2026 Tim O'Donnell. All rights not expressly granted here are reserved. |
| **Contact** | https://timodonnell.info |
| **Contribution Rate** | 2% of Covered Revenue |
| **Safeguard Cap** | 10% of Excess Income |
| **De Minimis** | Obligations under $25 in a year are waived |
| **Median Data Source** | U.S. Census Bureau, ACS 5-Year Estimates, Table B19013 (median household income), ZCTA level |
| **Governing Law** | [To be set on advice of counsel — see Appendix A] |

---

## 1. Definitions

**"Work"** — the software identified in the Parameters block.

**"You"** — any individual or entity exercising rights under this license.

**"Contribution Year"** — a calendar year, or, if you file taxes on a
different fiscal year, that fiscal year. All tests and obligations in this
license are computed independently for each Contribution Year.

**"Your Return"** — your federal income tax return for the Contribution
Year. For an entity, "Your Return" means the return of the natural person
holding the largest ownership interest in the entity. If ownership is so
dispersed that no natural person holds at least a 20% interest, the entity
is deemed Above the Line (Section 3) for every Contribution Year, and the
Safeguard Cap does not apply to it.

**"Your Income"** — the adjusted gross income (AGI) reported on Your
Return. If you were not required to file a return for the Contribution
Year, Your Income is deemed below the Local Median.

**"Your Zipcode"** — the ZIP code of the residence address cited on Your
Return for the Contribution Year. Because it comes from each year's return,
it updates automatically when you move.

**"Local Median"** — the median household income for Your Zipcode, from the
most recent edition of the Median Data Source published as of the last day
of the Contribution Year. If the figure for Your Zipcode is unpublished or
suppressed, use the median household income of the county containing your
residence instead. If the Median Data Source is discontinued, use the most
comparable successor dataset, chosen in good faith.

*Outside the United States:* substitute your country's equivalent annual
income filing for Your Return, and your country's national median household
income (from your national statistics agency, or the World Bank) for the
Local Median, converting currency at the Contribution Year's average
exchange rate. This is the roughest part of this draft; resolve all
ambiguity in your own favor (Section 7).

**"Excess Income"** — Your Income minus the Local Median, if positive;
otherwise zero.

**"Covered Revenue"** — gross revenue you received during the Contribution
Year that is attributable to the Work: revenue from selling or licensing
outputs created with the Work, from services performed using the Work, or
from products in which the Work or its outputs are a component. Where the
Work was one of several tools that contributed, estimate its share in good
faith. Money the Work did not help you earn is not Covered Revenue.

## 2. Grant of License

Subject to the conditions of this license, the Licensor grants you a
worldwide, non-exclusive license to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, and
distribute the Work, including commercially. Below the Line (Section 3)
this grant is royalty-free; Above the Line it is conditioned on the
Contribution in Section 4.

The Licensor additionally grants you a license under any patent claims the
Licensor holds that are necessarily infringed by the Work as provided, on
the same terms. No trademark rights are granted; you may not use the
Licensor's names or marks except to accurately describe the Work's origin.

Any copy or substantial portion of the Work you distribute must retain this
license, including the Parameters block, unmodified. Derivative works of
the Work must be distributed under this same license with the original
Parameters intact; you may additionally license **your own original
contributions** to a derivative under this license with your own Parameters
block, in which case the stacking rule in Section 5 governs the combined
obligation.

## 3. The Threshold Test — "the Line"

For each Contribution Year, exactly one of the following is true:

- **Below the Line** — Your Income ≤ Local Median. You owe **nothing** for
  that year, regardless of how much Covered Revenue you earned. Your use is
  royalty-free, fully licensed, and welcome.
- **Above the Line** — Your Income > Local Median. Section 4 applies.

The test repeats every year. Crossing the Line one year does not obligate
you in a later year when you are below it again.

## 4. The Contribution

If you are Above the Line for a Contribution Year, you owe the Licensor
the **lesser** of:

1. **Contribution Rate × Covered Revenue** for that year, and
2. **Safeguard Cap × Excess Income** for that year.

If the result is less than the De Minimis amount, it is waived.

Two consequences of this formula, stated plainly because they are the
point:

- **You can never be pulled below the Line by paying.** Your payment is
  capped at a fraction of the amount by which you exceed your local median,
  so paying it always leaves you above the median. The obligation phases in
  smoothly from zero — there is no cliff at the threshold, and growth is
  never punished.
- **If the Work earned you nothing, you owe nothing** — no matter how
  wealthy you are. Covered Revenue of zero means a contribution of zero.
  This is a royalty on benefit received, not a tax on existing.

### Worked examples (Rate 2%, Cap 10%)

*Example A — below the Line.* Local Median $68,400; Your Income $52,000.
You owe **$0**, even if the Work generated $200,000 in gross revenue for
you. (If you're netting $52,000 on that gross, you're not who this license
asks to pay.)

*Example B — just above the Line.* Local Median $68,400; Your Income
$79,000 → Excess Income $10,600. Covered Revenue $85,000.
Rate: 2% × $85,000 = $1,700. Cap: 10% × $10,600 = $1,060.
You owe the lesser: **$1,060** — and you remain comfortably above the
median after paying.

*Example C — well above the Line.* Local Median $68,400; Your Income
$310,000 → Excess Income $241,600. Covered Revenue $150,000.
Rate: 2% × $150,000 = $3,000. Cap: 10% × $241,600 = $24,160.
You owe **$3,000** — about 1% of your total income.

### Looking up your Local Median

Visit data.census.gov, search for table **B19013**, filter geography to
**ZCTA5** and enter your ZIP code. The most recent 5-Year Estimate is the
figure to use. It takes about two minutes, once a year, and only if you
think you might be Above the Line.

## 5. Payment, timing, and privacy

- Compute your contribution after filing Your Return; payment is due within
  90 days of filing, per instructions published at the Contact in the
  Parameters block.
- **You disclose nothing.** No tax returns, no income figures, no revenue
  reports. You self-assess and you pay a number. The Licensor never learns
  your income unless you choose to share it. A trust-based license should
  not demand the very privacy invasion that formal licenses use audits for.
- **If the Licensor cannot be paid** — no payment instructions are
  published, the Contact is dead, and reasonable effort finds no successor —
  you may satisfy the obligation by donating the amount to a registered
  charity of your choice, or, failing that, it is waived for that year.
  Software must not become a debt to a ghost.
- **Stacking.** If multiple works you use are licensed under Median Line
  License terms, your combined obligations under all of them for a
  Contribution Year are capped at the Safeguard Cap applied once, allocated
  pro-rata among licensors by their uncapped amounts. Adopting this license
  means accepting this rule — the ecosystem must never collectively drag a
  builder below the Line either.

## 6. Hardship

If paying would cause you genuine hardship the formula failed to
anticipate — a medical year, a disaster, a collapse after the tax year
closed — write to the Licensor and say so. The presumption is that the
waiver is granted. The formula serves the principle, not the other way
around.

## 7. Rule of Lenity

Every ambiguity in this license — in attribution of Covered Revenue, in
data-source edge cases, in non-U.S. application, in anything — is resolved
**in your favor**. A good-faith reading that benefits you is the correct
reading. This license would rather collect less than ever collect unjustly.

## 8. Good faith and termination

This license is enforced by trust, and trust is the intended mechanism.
However, the Licensor may terminate this license for a specific licensee
who is knowingly and materially evading a contribution they know to be
owed. Termination requires written notice describing the evasion and a
60-day opportunity to cure; payment or a good-faith dispute within that
period cures it. Termination is discretionary, is a last resort, and
requires actual bad faith — honest mistakes, good-faith estimates that turn
out low, and hardship are never grounds for termination.

## 9. Severability

If any provision of this license is held unenforceable, the remainder
stands. If the Contribution mechanism itself (Sections 3–5) is held
unenforceable in your jurisdiction, the license grant in Section 2
**survives, royalty-free**. The author of this license would rather give
the Work away than have a court turn it into something that takes from
people it promised not to take from.

## 10. No Warranty

THE WORK IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE, AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL
THE LICENSOR BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES, OR OTHER LIABILITY ARISING
FROM THE WORK OR ITS USE.

## 11. Versioning

The Licensor may publish future versions of this license. You may continue
under the version you received the Work under, or adopt any later version,
at your option.

## 12. For developers who want to use this license

Copy this document. Replace the Parameters block with your own project,
contact, and chosen Rate/Cap. Keep the structure: the annual Threshold
Test, the two-part lesser-of formula, the Rule of Lenity, the stacking
rule, and the hardship clause are what make it fair — the numbers are
yours to tune. If you harden it with real legal review, publish your
version openly so others can benefit.

## 13. Dedication of this license text

The text of this license (as distinct from the Work it covers) is dedicated
to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal
(creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0). Anyone may reproduce, adapt,
and build upon it, for any purpose, without permission.

